<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babylonian astronomy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as astronomy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babylonian astronomy refers to the systematic observation and mathematical prediction of celestial phenomena developed in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, flourishing under Chaldean patronage. Its records and methods influenced Greek astronomy after Alexander's conquests. The broader astronomical tradition was later continued by scholars in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, at observatories under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, and elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy.jpg" alt="Babylonian astronomy" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Linguica at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babylonian_tablet_recording_Halley%27s_comet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
